'''Tormentilla''' (tôr-men-til′ä̤), ''n.'' [NL. (Tournefort, 1700; earlier in Brunfels, 1530), < ML. ''tormentilla'': see ''tormentil''.] 1. A former genus of plants, now reduced to a section of ''Potentilla'', including those species which have the parts of the flowers in fours. The tormentil belongs to this section.—2. [''l. c.''] A plant of this subgenus; tormentil.

{{smaller block|This single yellow flower . . . is a ''tormentilla'', which is good against the plague.
{{right|''J. H. Shorthouse'', John Inglesant, iii.}}}}

'''tormentingly''' (tôr-men′ting-li), ''adv.'' In a tormenting manner; in a manner productive of suffering.

{{smaller block|He bounst and bet his head ''tormentingly''.
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Dan Bartholomew of Bath.}}}}

'''tormentingness''' (tôr-men′ting-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being tormenting. ''Bailey'', 1727.

'''tormentise†''', ''n.'' [ME., < ''torment'', ''v.''] Torment;

